st task first, keeping each to 4 requirements max and around 150-250 lines.\n\nSince implementation logically comes before tests, I'll propose the cmd/cat/main.go implementation task. I'm trying to figure out the correct PRD number for cat—the existing ones aren't strictly sequential, so I'll reference it by the directory pattern and the rel01.1 test suite.\n\nThe real constraint is that I need specific requirement IDs from the cat PRD to anchor the task properly, but I don't have access to that document's content yet. Let me look at the source_code section to see what's actually available. more carefully. The implementation_status says:\n- cmd/cat: spec complete (rel01.1), code at generation-merged tags\n\nSo the spec IS complete, meaning the PRD exists. But its content isn't in my context. I need to reference it for the stitch agent to read.\n\nGiven that I can't see the PRD content, I should reference it by the most likely path and structure requirements generically based on what cat does. But that violates \"Anchor every task to specific PRD requirement IDs.\"\n\nWait - maybe I should focus on something else. What about cmd/sponge (rel01.2)? Same problem - I don't have the PRD.\n\nLet me reconsider the overall state. The source_code section shows ALL existing code. Everything in source_code already exists. What doesn't exist: cmd/cat, cmd/sponge, and cmd/du - none of these are in source_code, though they all have specs complete. The earliest incomplete release after what exists is rel01.1 with cat.\n\nOK so let me think about this differently. The prompt says \"All project information is already provided in the project_context field above.\" But the PRD content for cat isn't included. That means I need to reference it as required_reading for the stitch agent. Looking at what cat typically does - it's a relatively simple utility that supports various flags for displaying file contents with options like numbering lines, showing special characters, and squeezing blank lines.\n\nI'm now examining the pattern from wc to understand how to structure the cat task requirements, looking at how individual requirements map to implementation tasks. Looking at the use case numbering pattern, cat would logically follow wc in the sequence, so the PRD would likely be prd006-cat or similar. Since I don't have the exact PRD ID, I'll reference the cat PRD from the product-requirements directory in the required_reading section so the stitch agent can locate it. I'm realizing I need to be more pragmatic here—I'll reference the cat PRD by its likely location in the product-requirements directory, and if the stitch agent can't pinpoint the exact file, it can search. For the task itself, I'll propose implementing the cat command, pulling in the relevant PRD, use case documentation, test suite, and existing patterns like the wc implementation as a reference, plus the differential testing utilities that would be needed. with prd006-cat as the most likely candidate, though I should note the agent should verify. Looking at the pattern across releases, wc is prd005 at rel01.0, cat is rel01.1, and sponge is rel01.2, which suggests cat might be prd006. The earlier PRDs map to rel00.0 tools, so prd004 could be ts, which is now at rel99.2.\n\nChecking the roadmap confirms the release structure: Batch 0 covers foundational infrastructure, Batch 1 starts with wc, then 1.1 through 1.3 add cat, sponge, and du respectively, while ts and the ls variants are deferred to rel99. This aligns with the vision phases where ts appears in Phase 1 alongside the core utilities.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what would be most useful for the stitch agent—proposing a well-structured cat implementation task that mirrors the wc pattern, with clear references to the PRD requirements and the necessary supporting materials like the test suite and existing code examples.\n\nThe real constraint I'm hitting is that without access to the actual PRD content, I can't anchor to specific requirement IDs as the guidelines demand. But the stitch agent will have filesystem access, so I can reference the PRD by its expected location and let it pull the actual requirement IDs from there. I'm looking at the requirement IDs from the existing wc PRD to understand the pattern—it has R1 through R6, and the test file references most of them. For cat, I'd expect similar requirements covering basic concatenation, line numbering, display flags, blank line handling, input sources, and error handling, but I can't just invent these IDs without the actual PRD. The constraint is clear: I need to derive everything from the PRD itself, not make assumptions about what the requirements should be.\n\nThe better approach is to reference the cat PRD directly in the required_reading section and anchor my requirements to specific PRD sections rather than guessing at requirement IDs. That way the stitch agent has the actual source material to work from instead of me making up what I think the requirements might be. on the standard structure\n\nBut I genuinely don't know the PRD ID numbers for cat. Let me go with what I can infer from the project patterns and from what cat does.\n\nActually, you know what, let me look at the PRD path pattern. They're at `docs/specs/product-requirements/prdNNN-name.yaml`. And looking at the existing code references:\n\n- prd001-testutils (R1-R5)\n- prd002-sys (R1-R2)\n- prd003-format (R1-R3)\n- prd005-wc (R1-R6)\n- prd011-magefiles (R1)\n\nSo the PRD for cat could be prd006, prd007, prd008, etc. Without more info, I'll go with prd006-cat as a reasonable guess.\n\nLet me look at this from the analysis/code_status section more carefully. I can see the release structure with use cases mapped out - there's rel00.0 with magefiles, format, sys, and testutils, then rel01.0 with wc, and rel01.1 with cat. So I have the use case `rel01.1-uc001-cat` but the PRD name isn't directly given.\n\nI think the best approach is to list the likely PRD path in required