ed, handling mixed file and stdin arguments. Finally, the output transformations cover line numbering where -b overrides -n, blank line squeezing, and end-of-line markers.\n\nFor error handling, I need to exit with code 1 if any file fails to open or read, printing errors to stderr in the format 'cat: <path>: <error>', but continue processing remaining files. The implementation should follow the run(args []string) int pattern from cmd/wc where main calls os.Exit(run(os.Args[1:])), and I'll use flag.NewFlagSet with SetOutput(os.Stderr) for proper flag error handling. I'm realizing D3 specifies bufio.NewWriter for output buffering, but that's not actually derived from the PRD or the architecture pattern I see in cmd/wc, which writes directly to stdout. The constraint is clear about not inventing design decisions, so I should either remove this detail or find a way to justify it from the requirements—but I think the safer move is to simplify D3 to focus only on what the PRD actually calls for.\n\nLet me revise the requirements to be more general and reference the PRD for specifics rather than prescribing implementation details I can't verify. I'll keep the core functionality clear: flag parsing with support for combination flags, file reading with stdin fallback, and all the output transformations the PRD defines.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the title constraint. Since I can't access the actual PRD file to get its exact number, I should anchor to the use case pattern instead and let the downstream agent resolve it to the proper PRD requirement ID. I'm settling on a task structure that references the cat PRD and related specs, with a single main.go file that implements the complete utility including flag parsing, file concatenation, output transformations, and error handling. The task touches just one file, which is reasonable for a self-contained utility implementation, and I've verified this doesn't duplicate anything already in the codebase. I need to add a file header